and uh jason  the reporter there joins me now and uh  jason well i've got to say it's one of  the most powerful pieces  of reporting uh in this pandemic just  give us a sense of the intensity you  found in there what struck you about  being in there  i think the first thing you notice is  people's faces the patients  obviously they're the only people's  faces you can see because everyone else  is masked up  and it's one of the things that we don't  really show you um  because we can't get consent and it's  very distressing to see  people there sometimes their eyes are  open but they can't really they're not  really seeing  if if you know what i mean sometimes  their eyes are closed and they're just  covered in tubes in their mouths  sometimes there's a tracheotomy  in the neck to support their breathing  and they all  look like they're gasping for breath  because  if it weren't for the machines they  wouldn't be alive  it's different in different areas so  there might be an icu which is very well  set up  and everything's spaced out and then in  other areas where they've had to adapt  toward  uh there might be it might be a bit more  cramped and my cameraman  andy porch described it as a as like a  field hospital  not he's been in one uh so he he should  know  but not because of the lack not because  of the lack of technology because that's  definitely there but just the atmosphere  and the feel  of people mucking in some of the rooms  are quite hot  feels very hot in the ppe and one of the  things that really struck me  going there was when i've been to  hospitals before it's often been with a  politician  on a campaign trail or something like  that and everything is very stage  managed  and you're told where to point the  camera and who to speak to they just  don't have time for any of that  it's it's it's everyone's mucking in uh  you you saw from the report people from  all over the hospital coming in to bring  in their specialties  uh there's no such thing as a comfort  zone for anybody  because even if they're in that  speciality they're in very different kit  from what they're used to  um and the other thing of course is that  there are people there who are not in  their specialties and you have  consultants  acting as nurses and you know we heard  from  matt hancock that there are 37 000  people  now in the uk hospitals there are  obviously a lot of people  uh there's a lot more lot more than they  used to um the more than ever in this  pandemic on on ventilators and we have  enough ventilators but you've got to  think of the amount of time it takes and  the amount of care  for people to keep those ventilators  functioning and the people on them  you know their eyes uh they need uh  moisturizing  um they they need the tubes clear  clearing they need the machines  monitoring so that all of that is going  on and that requires  people and they are tremendously  dedicated and  jason it's pretty rare i think that we  would run an interview  of someone who subsequently passed away  but uh  tony's widow linda you say wanted this  show  yeah thoughts again with uh tony and  his family and his widow linda he  clearly  thought about her an awful lot in in  those last moments and  um it was just by chance that i happened  to  walk into his world on his on his last  day  a full day alive and  we did the interview and he was actually  on his wa on the phone his wife  afterwards  and so we got his consent and then um he  he spoke to his wife about it  and i just called as i was editing it um  he'd given me his wife's number and i  called her up just to make sure that she  was okay with it it was only then  whilst i was editing the piece that uh  she told me that he had passed away  so you know i um i made sure that she  was okay with us using the interview and  she said if he wanted to do it  then then then i want you know i want it  to go out  um and i spoke to her today she hasn't  watched it  um she thought it would be too traumatic  but members of her family have  and she's very pleased that he's done it  she said he always wanted his 15 minutes  of fame  and she was pleased that he put that  message out there  about people staying safe and i think  she's very happy that he his 15 minutes  of fame  were well used yeah and jason i mean  you'll be doing some more reporting from  this hospital and that  i've seen the staff pretty keen to get  out their  uh message as well  yeah i mean that comes across doesn't it  each and every one of them are just  hoping  that we're living in two different  worlds really aren't we there's the  world outside of these hospitals where  everyone's getting extremely frustrated  and we all are  at the restrictions uh and the social  distancing and not being able to see  loved ones  and then there's a complete and those  people live in that world as well  and they don't get much time in it but  they do live in it and and they  understand  but um when you step into their world  and you see what they're going through  and we'll continue to do that because i  think it's important  now that journalists are you know  embedded in a way as we we just  sometimes are with the military when  we're  in a camp a military campaign i think we  have to be embedded with  uh these um these medics  you know to see what they're going  through and to give the public a window  into their worlds okay jason  thank you very much for being with us  thank you

